msgid ""
"Batch Renormalization: Towards Reducing Minibatch Dependence in Batch-"
"Normalized Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.03275)"
msgstr ""

#: marquetry.functions.normalization.batch_renormalization.BatchRenormalization:1
#: of
msgid "Apply batch renormalization to the input container."
msgstr ""

#: marquetry.functions.normalization.batch_renormalization.BatchRenormalization:3
#: of
msgid ""
"Batch renormalization extends batch normalization so that train-time "
"normalization stays close to the inference-time normalization even with "
"small or non-i.i.d. mini-batches. The batch statistics are corrected "
"toward the running statistics by the clipped factors ``r`` and ``d``:"
msgstr ""

#: marquetry.functions.normalization.batch_renormalization.BatchRenormalization:8
#: of
msgid ""
"``r = clip(sigma_batch / sigma_running, 1 / rmax, rmax)`` ``d = "
"clip((mu_batch - mu_running) / sigma_running, -dmax, dmax)``"
msgstr ""

#: marquetry.functions.normalization.batch_renormalization.BatchRenormalization:11
#: of
msgid ""
"and the output is ``y = gamma * (x_hat * r + d) + beta`` where ``x_hat`` "
"is the batch-normalized input. ``r`` and ``d`` are treated as constants "
"in the backward computation as defined in the paper."
msgstr ""

#: marquetry.functions.normalization.batch_renormalization.BatchRenormalization:15
#: of
msgid ""
"With ``rmax=1`` and ``dmax=0`` this is mathematically identical to "
":class:`marquetry.functions.BatchNormalization`."
msgstr ""

#: marquetry.functions.normalization.batch_renormalization.BatchRenormalization:20
#: of
msgid ""
"Generally, you don't need to execute ``forward`` and ``backward`` method "
"manually. You should use only ``__call__`` method."
msgstr ""

